BACKUP, RESTORE, AND RECOVERY INTEGRITY (APP-BACKUP-\*,
APP-RESTORE-\*) — EVERY APP

\================================================================================



(Complements DATA LIFECYCLE — BACKUP, RESTORE, AND
LEAVE above; does not replace it.)



APP-DATA-DOCUMENTS-ROOT [GATE]

- Desktop applications MUST store all user data, databases, business profiles, price books, templates, job libraries, blueprints, session settings, and exports under a dedicated, user-visible folder in the user's standard Documents directory (e.g. `Documents\N78-<AppName>\`), never hidden inside `AppData`, `LocalApplicationData`, `Roaming`, or hidden dot-directories.

- Standard consumer and enterprise backup systems (OneDrive, Windows File History, Time Machine, Carbonite, external drive sync) intentionally exclude `AppData`. Placing persistent application data or settings in `AppData` creates an unacceptable risk of silent data loss during system resets or PC migrations.

- Sibling desktop apps MUST include automatic migration on startup from legacy `AppData` locations into the `Documents` directory without data loss or user friction.



APP-BACKUP-ATOMIC [GATE]

\- A backup must be generated as an internally
consistent snapshot.

\- A failed or interrupted backup must not be presented
as a valid completed backup.



APP-BACKUP-INTEGRITY [GATE]

\- Backups must include sufficient integrity protection
to detect corruption or unauthorized modification

  before
restore.



APP-RESTORE-VALIDATE-FIRST [GATE]

\- Validate backup structure, version, integrity, and
required security metadata before replacing or

  modifying
current application data.



APP-RESTORE-NO-DATA-LOSS [GATE]

\- A failed restore must not unnecessarily destroy the
user's existing valid data.



APP-RESTORE-RECOVERY [QA]

\- Products supporting restore must document recovery
behavior for wrong credentials, corrupt backups,

  unsupported
versions, and interrupted restore operations.





\================================================================================

UPGRADES AND SCHEMA MIGRATION HARDENING
(APP-UPGRADE-\*) — EVERY APP

\================================================================================



(Complements SCHEMA VERSION AND MIGRATION above; does
not replace it.)



APP-UPGRADE-SUPPORTED-PATH [QA]

\- Each release must identify the supported upgrade
paths from prior production versions and verify

  those paths
before release.



APP-UPGRADE-INTERRUPTION [GATE]

\- Critical migrations and upgrades must be designed so
that interruption does not leave the application

  in an
unrecoverable state.



APP-UPGRADE-BACKUP [GATE]

\- Before destructive or materially irreversible
migration operations, create or preserve an appropriate

  recovery point
when technically feasible.



APP-UPGRADE-DOWNGRADE [QA]

\- Document whether downgrade is supported. If
downgrade is unsafe, the application must prevent or

  clearly warn
against it rather than silently corrupting data.





\================================================================================

DATA LIFECYCLE HONESTY (APP-DATA-LIFECYCLE / DELETION
/ TEMPORARY / EXPORT-TRUTH)

\================================================================================



(Complements DATA LIFECYCLE — BACKUP, RESTORE, AND
LEAVE and APP-DATA-PORTABILITY / OPEN-EXPORT.)



APP-DATA-LIFECYCLE [QA]

\- Each product must document the lifecycle of material
user data: creation, storage, use, export,

  backup,
retention, and deletion.



APP-DATA-DELETION [QA]

\- User-facing deletion operations must clearly
identify what is deleted and must not falsely imply

  deletion of
copies that remain in user-controlled exports or backups.



APP-DATA-TEMPORARY [GATE]

\- Temporary files, caches, intermediate exports, and
working copies must not retain sensitive data

  indefinitely.



APP-DATA-EXPORT-TRUTH [QA]

\- Export documentation and UI must accurately state
what data is included, excluded, transformed, or omitted.

